><text color="#9643FC" creationdate="D:20251112111806+10'00'" flags="print,nozoom,norotate" inreplyto="f5e8764b-bc69-4e5c-be7e-1a09ec647932" date="D:20251112111806+10'00'" name="6c442ae9-c8cc-40ee-95c5-dbb44de9f163" icon="Comment" page="8" rect="51.162964,625.901306,75.162964,649.901306" subject="Sticky Note" title="alexd"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Although swimming technique was the greater contributor for&#xD;changes in performance of T400 over the macrocycle, it is&#xD;closely connected with energetics; thus, a powerful and endurable&#xD;metabolic base cannot be overlooked. It is important to be&#xD;aware of the growth effects because late to postpubertal agegroup&#xD;swimmers still are sensitive to training stimulus. Coaches&#xD;and performance analysts should be aware that training&#xD;periodization is a dynamic process, with a variable pattern of&#xD;changes in key technical, energetic, and anthropometric characteristics.&#xD;Swimmers should be evaluated regularly by coaches and&#xD;sports scientists over the training season.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="8" rect="612.000000,557.901306,796.000000,649.901306"
/></text
